\chapter{Section Markers: Mapping Code to Source}
\label{ch:languages}

The code you compile is \emph{tangled output} --- files weft assembles
from scraps scattered across many concept (\verb|.weft|) files. So a line
in \verb|server.js| does not correspond to any file you wrote; it was
built from fragments defined in \verb|routes.weft|, \verb|database.weft|,
and others. To keep that connection, weft writes \emph{section markers}
into the generated code: small comments that name the scrap and point at
the exact source file and line it came from.

These markers are the backbone of source mapping. They let the
\textbf{programmer} browse generated code and jump back to the narrative;
they let the \textbf{compiler and debugger} report against the
\verb|.weft| source (via \verb|#line|); and they let \textbf{tools and AI
agents} navigate mechanically between output and source (Chapter~\ref{ch:ai}).
Because a marker is written as a \emph{comment}, weft must know each
file's comment style --- and that is the one reason it detects the
language.

\section{What a marker looks like}

Each scrap is wrapped in an opening marker (scrap number + source
location) and a closing marker (number only), in the language's comment
syntax. When fragments nest, each expansion gets its own pair, forming a
clear hierarchy:

\begin{quote}
\begin{tabular}{@@{}ll@@{}}
\verb|{|\,\ldots\ / \verb|{:|\,\ldots & Opening / closing delimiter. \\
Number & Scrap ID, unique across the whole document. \\
\verb|file:line| & Source \verb|.weft| file and line (opening only). \\
\end{tabular}
\end{quote}

\noindent The same scrap, tangled to JavaScript, Python, and C:

\begin{quote}
\begin{verbatim}
// {3: routes.weft:42}
app.get("/api/users", handler);
// {:3}

# {5: models.weft:31}
class User: ...
# {:5}

#line 12 "memory.weft"
/* {2: memory.weft:12} */
void *safe_malloc(size_t n) { ... }
/* {:2} */
\end{verbatim}
\end{quote}

\noindent For C-family languages weft also emits a \verb|#line| directive
above the marker, so the compiler itself reports errors against the
\verb|.weft| file. Scrap numbers are document-global and stable as long
as you do not add or remove \verb|@@{...@@}| blocks before them.
Chapter~\ref{ch:debug} shows how to \emph{use} all this while debugging.

\section{How weft knows the comment style}

To write a marker as a comment, weft needs the file's comment style. It
finds it by a short chain:

\begin{quote}
file extension $\longrightarrow$ language $\longrightarrow$ comment style
$\longrightarrow$ markers in the tangled output
\end{quote}

\noindent \verb|@@o server.js| has extension \verb|.js|; weft looks
\verb|js| up in its language table, sees JavaScript uses \verb|//|, and
wraps each scrap accordingly. \verb|@@o main.c| matches \verb|c|
(\verb|/* */| plus \verb|#line|); \verb|@@o config.py| matches \verb|py|
(\verb|#|). No flags are needed --- the extension alone is enough.

\section{Overriding the language with \texttt{@@l}}

When the extension is not enough --- a \verb|.txt| file that is really SQL
--- put \verb|@@l| \textit{tag} on the line \emph{before} the directive:

\begin{quote}
\begin{verbatim}
@@l sql
@@o queries.txt
@@{SELECT * FROM users;
@@}
\end{verbatim}
\end{quote}

\noindent Now the markers in \verb|queries.txt| use \verb|--| comments.
\verb|@@l| works on \verb|@@d| fragments too, and the tag it sets drives
both the section markers and the Markdown highlighting. When several
sources of language information exist, weft resolves them in order:
explicit per-file flags on \verb|@@o| win, then an \verb|@@l| tag, then
the file extension.

\section{Defining new languages with \texttt{@@L}}
\label{sec:atL}

The built-in table is large but cannot know every language or house
style. \verb|@@L| registers a new mapping --- or overrides an existing one
--- in the preamble:

\begin{quote}
{\tt @@L} \textit{name style} {\rm[}{\tt +d}{\rm]}
\end{quote}

\noindent where \textit{name} is the tag recognised in \verb|@@l| and file
extensions, \textit{style} is the comment syntax (\verb|//|, \verb|#|,
\verb|--|, \verb|/*|, or \verb|<!--|), and \verb|+d| optionally enables
\verb|#line| directives. For example:

\begin{quote}
\begin{verbatim}
@@L wgsl //       % WebGPU shading language: // comments
@@L c //          % override the built-in: // instead of /* */
\end{verbatim}
\end{quote}

\noindent User entries are searched \emph{before} the built-in table (up
to 32 of them); \verb|@@L| is processed in pass~1 and skipped when weaving.

\section{The language table}
\label{sec:langtable}

weft ships with over 250 entries --- languages, extensions, and aliases
--- grouped by comment style. A tag is matched against \verb|@@l| tags and
file extensions alike:

\begin{quote}
\small
\begin{tabular}{lp{8.6cm}}
\hline
Comment style & Tags (representative) \\
\hline
\verb|/* */| + \verb|#line| & c, h, cpp, cc, cxx, hpp, objc, m, mm, cu, cuh \\[2pt]
\verb|/* */| & css, scss, sass, less, cl \\[2pt]
\verb|//| & js, ts, jsx, tsx, java, scala, kotlin, go, rust, rs, zig, swift, dart, php, graphql, proto, sol, glsl, wgsl \\[2pt]
\verb|#| & python, py, sh, bash, perl, ruby, r, awk, makefile, cmake, julia, nim, elixir, yaml, toml, dockerfile, terraform, nix, ini \\[2pt]
\verb|--| & lua, sql, psql, haskell, hs, elm, ada, vhdl, lean, agda, idris, erlang \\[2pt]
\verb|<!-- -->| & html, xml, svg, xaml, erb, hbs, twig, md, rst, tex, latex \\[2pt]
(none) & json, csv, tsv, txt, diff, patch \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{quote}

\noindent If a tag is in neither table, weft falls back to \verb|//| with
no \verb|#line|. Tags mapped to the ``none'' style (\verb|json|,
\verb|txt|, \ldots) produce no markers at all.

\section{Suppressing markers}
\label{sec:suppress}

Markers are a help, not an obligation. You suppress them when comments
would be wrong or unwanted --- a data file, a format with no comment
syntax, or generated output you want kept pristine:

\begin{description}
\item[Per file] Put \verb|-s| on the \verb|@@o| line
(\S\ref{sec:fileflags}). The language is still detected (so Markdown
highlighting works), but no section markers or \verb|#line| directives are
written for that file.
\item[Mid-line references] A fragment referenced \emph{after} visible text
on a line is spliced with no markers at all, so it reads as inline text
--- \verb|int port = @@<port@@>;| becomes \verb|int port = 4000;|. A
reference at the start of a line is a block expansion and gets markers
normally. This is what makes fragments usable as inline values; see
Chapter~\ref{ch:examples}.
\item[``none'' languages] Tags like \verb|json| and \verb|txt| have no
comment style, so they never get markers.
\end{description}

\section{A note on highlighting}

The language tag has a second, minor use: in the Markdown weave
(\verb|-w md|), weft puts it on the code element ---
\verb|<code class="language-python">| --- so GitHub and client-side
highlighters (highlight.js, Prism) colour the code. In the \LaTeX\ weave,
the \verb|listings| package highlights regardless. Highlighting is a
by-product of knowing the language; the section markers are the point.
